uestions carl  i'd like to share this gentleman's  skepticism and  raise a counter argument and see what  your response would be  i have to paraphrase it but i i think  people remember the remark i believe it  was in kennedy's inaugural address  where he said something about uh  in the preservation of freedom we would  you know fight every battle i think you  know the quote  um if he says something like that  he then participates in this coup which  maybe it didn't produce the result they  thought but nonetheless it was very  clear what our involvement was in this  thing  and then things begin to disintegrate in  vietnam as they did after this thing  occurred  how could we then walk away from this i  mean it would seem to me that the  entire credibility of the u.s government  not just in vietnam but credibility in  general  would be demolished if something like  this would have we just kind of  did this how could they kind of how  could we contemplate that if that's what  happened and we know that is what  happened after this coup i know that the  the vital interest of the us throughout  this is what dean rush talked about the  credibility the credibility gap that  developed later and so forth but this is  again before the gulf of tonkin this is  before all those events later on and  president kennedy i'm convinced thought  that we will cut it back to an advisory  level we'll try to hang on as long as we  can there and maybe that will work and  if it doesn't work then we're going to  have to just call it a wash i know i  know that doesn't fit i know i've  wrestled with that over and over and i  can't i can't make it all come together  yet in my mind i know  would you comment on maxwell taylor's  relationship with president kennedy and  his relationship with the other joint  chiefs and how they affected each effect  in his policy recommendations  well maxwell taylor was  as far as i could tell about the only  general that president kennedy liked  he curtis lemay  he 